In Fig. 2, the action posture estimation network consists of three parts: fixed space GCN, dynamic spatiotemporal GCN, and second-order connected adjacency matrix GCN. The fixed space GCN is composed of traditional GCN structures, and the adjacency matrix of the human skeleton describes the fixed connection relationships between various key points. However, under the complex spatiotemporal pose changes in sports videos, the potential motion relationships between key points are not directly connected through fixed skeleton structures. Relying solely on fixed connection relationships will limit the processing power of GCN [19, 20]. Therefore, different dynamic GCNs are constructed based on the different motion states of key points. Firstly, the distance information of key points in space and the movement speed in time position are integrated to dynamically learn the relationship between different key points and construct dynamic spatiotemporal GCN.
